From 937c4fa7b91f13d474b9518dbcee2b470c16dd14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 00:57:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(entities): synchronize authoritative entity references

Translate explicit native reference clearing into Java metadata instead of
leaving the previous owner or attack target active. Include elder guardians
and preserve full-width negative actor IDs.

BDS 1.26.51.1 build 51061372, protocol 2193, uses guardian TARGET 0 and
wither head target -1 as empty values. Untamed wolves have OWNER -1.
Java 26.3 uses zero entity IDs and nullable owner UUIDs for empty references.

Retain unresolved references by source lifetime and target unique ID. Clear
the Java value immediately when the target is unavailable, resolve only
after its Java spawn packet, and clear dependent metadata on despawn without
erasing native state. Superseded references and removed source lifetimes
cannot resurrect. Index dependents and coalesce changed fields per source
instead of scanning every actor on each spawn.

Stateful regressions exercise real metadata updates, tracker spawn/removal
hooks, and encoded Java metadata. Cover restoration, owner reassignment,
late arrival, repeated spawn, independent wither heads, target unload and
return, superseded references, and source replacement.

Preserve projectile OWNER in standard Java spawn data when its actor exists.
An independently advertised ownership channel carries late resolution,
changes, and clearing to capable clients; ordinary Java clients retain the
standard spawning path. A UUID for each Java spawn lifetime prevents native
actor-ID reuse from changing a replacement projectile's owner.

Actual strict-BDS small fireballs carry full-width negative OWNER IDs for
their blaze shooters. Java 26.3 reconstructs projectile owners from spawn
data but has no subsequent owner update packet. Keep decoding, reference
resolution, channel gating, and frontend lifecycle indexing in core. Client
setOwner integration remains in a separate add-on change.

Cover real spawn handling across projectile families, late capability
registration, replacement, unload and return, explicit clears, malformed
payloads, and both frontend arrival orders. Visible native comparisons and
complete projectile action behavior remain required.

Translate fishing-hook TARGET to standard Java HOOKED_ENTITY metadata.
BDS 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 sends zero for no target and signed native
actor IDs after a genuine rod cast. Java 26.3 stores Java entity ID plus
one. Retain native identity through late arrival and target unload, and
clear Java attachment when either actor is removed. This uses ordinary
Java metadata on direct and ViaProxy connections.
